				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1705184); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1705184&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606834601" alt="Mortos" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1705184)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1705184); return false;" >Mortos</a></span> <span class="odate time_1553469240 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Mar 2019 23:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4195901">
									

<p>There's a lot here. I apologise if some of it is unclear; I'm pretty tired right now but if I didn't do it now I'd just forget.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Lots&nbsp;of&nbsp;notes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>fresh supply of square papers</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>most of its time searching for papers</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Only papers below 50&#160;g/m²</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In each of these cases it should be &quot;paper&quot;, no &quot;s&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>square papers with a dimension of 15 x 15&#160;cm</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This would be cleaner as &quot;with dimensions of 15 x 15&#160;cm&quot;, but you could also just trim it right down and say &quot;paper measuring 15 x 15&#160;cm&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Only papers below 50&#160;g/m² can be given to SCP-4393 to slow down the rate of degradation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Adding a comma after &quot;SCP-4393&quot; will make this easier to read.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>may cause unforeseen irreversible damages.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Should be &quot;damage&quot;, no &quot;s&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4393 is a sentient, origami crane</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No comma is necessary here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4393 uses it two, relatively manipulative</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove the comma here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and will be collectively referred to as SCP-4393-1 instances.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can remove the word &quot;instances&quot; here. It's not necessarily wrong, but it's just a little redundant and the sentence reads better without it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Destroying an SCP-4393-1 instance or when SCP-4393 is handled by any personnel triggers an aggressive behavior</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole fragment here is a little clunky to read. It would be better phrased as something like &quot;Directly handling SCP-4393 or destroying an instance of SCP-4939-1 triggers an aggressive response[&#8230;]&quot;<br />
This also removes the word &quot;behaviour&quot;, which is used again later in the sentence. Repeated words like this can sometimes make a sentence read fairly awkwardly, so it helps the whole thing flow a bit better without it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>towards the personnel responsible for any of the conditions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can remove the &quot;for any of the conditions&quot; part here. It's a little self-reinforcing, which is unnecessary since it's still part of the same sentence describing the situation in the first place.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, due to their lack of substantial physical strength</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No need for the word &quot;substantial&quot; here. It's an unnecessary qualifier as it implies that substantial strength is required to be a threat (which clearly isn't the case as these things become very dangerous later on in the article.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Most of the contained SCP-4393-1 instances will continuously collide itself with the containment walls, possibly as an attempt to escape. The instance will repeat this behavior until it becomes immobilized due to crumpling.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There are a few issues with these 2 sentences. The &quot;Most of the&quot; part makes the whole thing feel unclinical, use of the word &quot;itself&quot; in &quot;continuously collide itself with&quot; is incorrect as the sentence already has a subject (&quot;the contained SCP-4393-1 instances&quot;), and then in the second sentence you begin to refer to them by the singular.<br />
Consider rewording to something like &quot;Contained SCP-4393-1 instances will continually collide with the walls of their containment cell, possibly in an attempt to escape. Instances will repeat this behaviour until they are immobilized by the damage caused.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If SCP-4393 is prevented from creating any SCP-4393-1 instances, the entity will display self-destructive behavior such as repeatedly flying into walls and personnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You don't need the word &quot;any&quot; in here as it adds an odd condition to the statement. You can also just say &quot;it&quot; instead of &quot;the entity&quot;, as it is already the subject of the sentence.<br />
Additionally, and this isn't wrong but just a thought, since you already described this behaviour for the -1 instances, you could just refer back to it here. For example, &quot;If SCP-4393 is prevented from creating SCP-4939-1 instances, it will display self-destructive behaviour similar to that of previously created instances in containment.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Restricting its movement via trapping it between a heavy object or attaching it to an adhesive surface also triggers the destructive behavior, resulting in tears and further degradation particularly at its creases.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think you need all of these examples in here, they just kind of bloat out the sentence. You can just say &quot;Restricting its movement also triggers destructive behaviour, resulting in tears[&#8230;]&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Possibly due to substantial degradation, SCP-4393 ceased movement on 23/5/1958 at exactly 19:55 and was confirmed to be neutralized. Prior to this, SCP-4393 was halfway done folding another SCP-4393-1 instance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Beginning the sentence with speculation rather than the actual subject of the sentence makes it feel indirect and slow-paced. The second sentence here also reads very unclinically (phrases like &quot;halfway done&quot; aren't clinical.) Consider moving &quot;possibly due to substantial degradation&quot; from the start of the sentence to the end, and replace &quot;halfway done&quot; with something like &quot;in the process of&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After seven seconds had passed, all instances were observed to begin shaking</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unless it specifically being seven seconds is important, I'd just say &quot;Several seconds later&quot;, otherwise you might give the impression that it specifically being seven seconds has some significance. Additionally, they actually <em>did</em> begin shaking, so you don't need the &quot;were observed to&quot; qualifier. It actually happened, so you can just say &quot;all instances began shaking&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>finished the uncompleted origami crane which immediately gained sentience.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Add a comma between &quot;crane&quot; and &quot;which&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All the SCP-4393-1 instances began</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can remove the word &quot;all&quot; here. It's already clear you are referring to all of them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The aggregate momentarily collapsed in on itself creating a loud shockwave, composed of multiple crane calls which instantaneously caused all paper</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You've got the comma in the wrong place here; move it from after &quot;shockwave&quot; to after &quot;calls&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>began to move in a westerly direction from the site and accelerate at an unprecedented speed of 1200 kph</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This reads a little incredulously, which isn't something you want in a clinical document. Additionally, &quot;accelerate&quot; should be &quot;accelerating&quot;, but I don't think you mean acceleration here, I think you just mean &quot;moving&quot;. ie &quot;began to move in a westerly direction at a speed of 1200kp/h&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4393-1 instances began mobbing on the aircraft</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove the word &quot;on&quot; here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>resulting in the destruction of three of the aircraft due to engine failure and severely damaging the other two which were ordered to retreat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Due to the repeated use of the word &quot;aircraft&quot; here this reads a little oddly. Consider rewording it to something like &quot;SCP-4393-1 instances began mobbing the aircraft, destroying three of them and severely damaging the other two, which were ordered to retreat.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>witnesses were amnesticed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Generally &quot;amnesticised&quot; is used in this case.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

